  "text": "Three more, a sentence or two each. dbt is a sequel transformation tool: it runs select statements in the warehouse, and saves each result as a table or a view. Its ref calls tell it what each model depends on, so it builds them in order, with tests and docs alongside. It's the T in ELT. The fan-out problem: one upstream change sets off many downstream recomputations. Correct a dimension table, and thousands of aggregates built on it go stale. Recompute only the affected partitions, let the dependency graph find the smallest scope, and use CDC so only the change travels. Data lineage tracks where data came from and what transformed it, from source system to dashboard. It answers why a metric changed, what breaks if you drop a column, and where personal data went, for compliance. Each answer here assumed one machine could hold the data. When a day of events outgrows it, the work has to split across many. Next: distributed processing.",
